update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(v.b)) from (values (1,$q$Read the short story "The Gift of the Magi" by O. Henry (public domain). Then read this lesson.

Every story is built on one idea: somebody wants something, and something stands in the way. Plot is the order of events in a story, and it has a shape. Exposition introduces the characters and setting. Rising action builds the trouble. The climax is the turning point, where everything changes. Falling action shows what follows, and the resolution shows how things settle.

Conflict is the struggle that drives the plot. External conflict is a struggle against something outside the character, such as another person, nature or society. Internal conflict is a struggle inside the character, such as fear against courage or temptation against conscience. Example from Scripture: in 1 Samuel 17, David faces Goliath. The external conflict is a shepherd boy against a giant. The internal conflict is David choosing faith over fear. Good readers notice both.

How to read for plot, ask three questions. What does the main character want? What is in the way? What changes at the turning point? If you can answer all three, you understand the plot.

Your task: (1) Name the conflict in the story. (2) Mark the climax. (3) Say whether the main conflict is external or internal, and explain why in one sentence.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.1$q$),(2,$q$Read "The Gift of the Magi" again, this time watching the characters.

Characterization is how an author shows us who a character is. Direct characterization is when the narrator tells us outright, such as "she was a generous woman." Indirect characterization is when the author shows us through what the character says, does and thinks, and through how others treat that character. If a character gives up his lunch so a friend can eat, we do not need to be told he is kind. His action shows us.

Learn four labels. A round character is complex, with strengths and weaknesses. A flat character shows only one or two traits. A dynamic character changes over the story. A static character stays the same. Scripture gives a fine example in Peter. He speaks boldly, acts quickly, fails, weeps and is restored. Peter is round and dynamic, and the Gospels show this through his words and actions more than they tell it.

Method: pick a character. Write what the character says, does and thinks, and what others say about him or her. Sum the person up in one sentence, then back that sentence with evidence from the text.

Your task: choose one character and list three details that reveal who he or she is. Label each detail direct or indirect. Then say whether the character is round or flat, and dynamic or static.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.1$q$),(3,$q$Read Genesis chapter 22, verses 1 to 14 in the King James Version, and notice the setting.

Setting is the time and place of a story. Mood is the feeling the reader gets from it. Authors build mood through details of place, weather, sound and light. Setting can also shape the conflict. A story set in a storm or a desert feels very different from one set in a garden at dawn, even when the events are the same.

Look at the setting of Genesis 22. Abraham rises early in the morning, saddles his donkey, and travels three days to a mountain in the land of Moriah. He leaves the young men and the donkey behind and climbs with Isaac, carrying wood and fire. The long journey, the early hour and the lonely mountain build a mood of quiet tension and solemn waiting. The setting also shapes the conflict, because the test happens far from home where no one else can help.

Method: list place details, time details and sensory details. Then ask what feeling they create together, and which words create it.

Your task: (1) Describe the setting of Genesis 22:1 to 14 in two sentences. (2) Name the mood. (3) Point to two words or details that create the mood.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.1$q$),(4,$q$Read Luke chapter 15, verses 11 to 32, the parable of the prodigal son (King James Version).

Theme is the central message about life that a story expresses. It is a full statement, not a single word. "Forgiveness" is a topic. "A loving father welcomes back even a son who has wasted everything" is a theme. To find the theme, ask what the main character learns, what changes, and what lesson the story seems to teach about people or about God.

Point of view is who tells the story. In first person the narrator is a character and uses "I." In third person limited the narrator follows one character's thoughts. In third person omniscient the narrator knows every character's thoughts. The point of view decides what the reader can know. In Luke 15 the narrator tells the story from outside, and we see into both sons, so we learn the older brother's anger as well as the younger brother's hunger.

Method: state what happens in one sentence, then ask what the story teaches. Make sure your theme could be true outside this story.

Your task: (1) State the theme of Luke 15:11 to 32 in one full sentence. (2) Name the point of view. (3) Give one detail from the text that supports your theme.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.2, ELA.9.R.1.3$q$),(5,$q$Read Psalm 1 (King James Version) and "The Road Not Taken" by Robert Frost (public domain).

A poem is organized in lines and stanzas. A line is one row of words. A stanza is a group of lines, like a paragraph in prose. The speaker is the voice in the poem, and it may not be the poet. Poems are often short but dense, so they reward slow reading.

Read a poem three times. First read for sound, aloud if you can, and listen to the rhythm. Second read for meaning, and ask what is happening and what the speaker thinks or feels. Third read for craft, and notice the images, repeated words and the way the stanzas are shaped. Psalm 1 contrasts the blessed man who delights in the law of the LORD with the ungodly who are like chaff. Frost's poem shows a traveler choosing between two roads in a wood. Both poems are about choices, but they approach them differently.

Your task: for the poem by Robert Frost, write down (1) the speaker, (2) the subject, and (3) the number of stanzas and lines in each stanza. Then write one sentence telling how the choice in Psalm 1 is similar to or different from the choice in Frost's poem.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.4$q$),(6,$q$Read Psalm 19, verses 1 to 6 (King James Version).

Imagery is language that appeals to the senses: sight, sound, smell, taste and touch. Strong poets paint pictures so we feel what they describe. Sound devices add music. Rhyme repeats ending sounds. Alliteration repeats beginning sounds, as in "slippery slope." Assonance repeats vowel sounds. Rhythm is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables.

Psalm 19:1 says, The heavens declare the glory of God; and the firmament sheweth his handywork. Day and night are pictured as speakers who pour out speech and show knowledge, even though there is no voice heard. In verses 4 to 6 the sun comes out like a bridegroom from his chamber, and rejoices as a strong man to run a race. Those are images of sight and of motion, and verse 6 adds heat. Notice how a poet can make us feel a sunrise without using a single modern word.

Method: read slowly and underline every phrase that makes a picture in your mind. Beside each one, write the sense it appeals to.

Your task: (1) Find three images in Psalm 19:1 to 6 and name the sense each one appeals to. (2) Find one sound device and name it.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.4, ELA.9.R.3.1$q$),(7,$q$Read Psalm 23 (King James Version).

Figurative language means words used beyond their plain meaning. A simile compares two things using like or as. A metaphor says one thing is another. A symbol is an object or image that stands for a larger idea. Personification gives human qualities to things that are not human, such as "the sea whispered." These tools let writers say much in few words.

Psalm 23 opens with a metaphor: The LORD is my shepherd. David does not say God is like a shepherd. He says God is a shepherd, and the rest of the psalm shows what that means. A shepherd feeds, guides, protects and stays close. Green pastures and still waters stand for rest and provision. The valley of the shadow of death stands for the darkest trouble. The rod and the staff stand for protection and guidance. The cup that runneth over stands for abundance.

Method: when you find a figure of speech, name it, say what two things are being compared, and explain the feeling or idea it adds.

Your task: (1) Find one metaphor in Psalm 23 and explain what it means in your own words. (2) Find one symbol and tell what it stands for.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.3.1$q$),(8,$q$Read Psalm 23 again, and compare it with "The Road Not Taken."

This lesson puts your skills together. When you study a poem, work through the same checklist each time. Who is the speaker? What is the subject? How are the lines and stanzas arranged? What images appeal to the senses? What sound devices do you hear? What figures of speech appear? Finally, what does the poem mean as a whole?

Apply the checklist to Psalm 23. The speaker is a believer talking about and then to God. The subject is trust in God's care. The images are pastures, waters, a dark valley and a table prepared. The metaphor of the shepherd runs through the whole poem. The poem moves from "he" in the first verses to "thou" in the valley, as the speaker draws closer to God in the hard place. Compare Frost's poem. Both poems use a journey as a picture of life, but Psalm 23 ends in comfort and belonging while Frost ends in reflection on a choice.

A strong paragraph about a poem names the device, quotes a short piece of the text, and explains the effect on the reader.

Your task: write a paragraph of 100 to 150 words explaining how two poetic devices help Psalm 23 comfort its reader. Quote at least one phrase from the psalm.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.1.4, ELA.9.R.3.1$q$)) as v(p,b), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p;

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(v.b)) from (values (9,$q$Read the Gettysburg Address by Abraham Lincoln (public domain), about 270 words.

Informational writing follows patterns called text structures. Description lists features of a topic. Sequence puts events in time order. Compare and contrast shows likenesses and differences. Cause and effect explains why things happen. Problem and solution names a difficulty and a way through it. Signal words help you spot the structure: first, next, then, however, similarly, because, therefore, as a result, one solution.

Knowing the structure helps you predict what comes next and remember what you read. The Gettysburg Address begins with the past: "Four score and seven years ago." It moves to the present, where the nation is engaged in a great civil war and has come to dedicate a field. It ends by looking to the future and the work that remains. That time movement, from past to present to future, shows a sequence pattern. It also contains a problem, whether the nation can endure, and a call to act.

Method: read once for the main point, then read again and circle signal words. Ask which pattern those words fit best.

Your task: (1) Name the main structure of the Gettysburg Address. (2) List three signal words or phrases that helped you decide. (3) Tell whether a second structure also appears.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.2.1$q$),(10,$q$Read Abraham Lincoln's Second Inaugural Address, the final paragraph beginning "With malice toward none" (public domain), and Ecclesiastes 3:1 to 8 (King James Version).

The central idea is the most important point a text makes about its topic. Supporting details are the facts, examples and reasons that develop it. The central idea is not the topic, which is a word or short phrase, and it is not a small detail. It is a full statement of what the author wants you to understand.

Ecclesiastes 3:1 gives the central idea: To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven. Verses 2 to 8 are the supporting details, listing pairs of times: a time to be born and a time to die, a time to weep and a time to laugh, a time to keep silence and a time to speak. Together they show that life has many seasons and each has its purpose. Lincoln's closing paragraph also has a central idea: after a long war the nation should heal with charity and justice for all.

Method: ask what the text is mostly about, then what the author says about it. Test your sentence by asking whether the details prove it.

Your task: (1) Write the central idea of Ecclesiastes 3:1 to 8 in one sentence. (2) List two supporting details. (3) Write the central idea of Lincoln's paragraph in one sentence.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.2.2$q$),(11,$q$Read the first six paragraphs of Frederick Douglass, "What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?" (public domain), using the excerpt linked in the readings above.

Author's purpose is the reason an author writes: to inform, to persuade, to entertain or to express. Many texts have more than one purpose, but one is usually primary. Perspective is the author's point of view, shaped by experience and beliefs. Word choice, tone and what the author includes or leaves out reveal both.

Douglass spoke on July 5, 1852, to an audience that came to celebrate American freedom. He had been born into slavery and escaped. Notice how he begins with respect for the founders and then turns sharply to the question of whom the celebration serves. His purpose is to persuade the audience that a nation that celebrates liberty cannot honestly ignore slavery. His perspective as a formerly enslaved man gives him credibility, and his tone moves from careful courtesy to moral urgency.

Method: ask who is speaking, to whom, and why. Then underline words that show tone, and note anything the author seems to stress or leave out.

Your task: (1) State the purpose of the Douglass speech. (2) State his perspective. (3) Give two pieces of evidence from the text for each answer.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.2.3$q$),(12,$q$Read the Gettysburg Address and the final paragraph of Lincoln's Second Inaugural Address again.

A summary is a short version of the main ideas in your own words. It leaves out small details and opinions. A paraphrase restates a passage in your own words at about the same length, keeping every idea. Neither one is a copy. If you copy words directly, you must use quotation marks and give credit.

To summarize, find the central idea, choose the most important supporting points, and write them briefly. A sample summary of the Gettysburg Address might say that Lincoln honors the soldiers who died, says the living must finish the work they began, and calls on the nation to renew its commitment to freedom and self government.

To compare two texts, look at topic, purpose, structure, tone and central idea. The Gettysburg Address is short and solemn, and honors the dead. The Second Inaugural is longer and reflects on the causes of the war and on God's judgment, ending in a call to heal. They share a concern for the nation's future but differ in tone and focus.

Your task: (1) Write a three sentence summary of the Gettysburg Address in your own words. (2) Write one sentence telling how it differs from the Second Inaugural in tone.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.3.2, ELA.9.R.3.3$q$),(13,$q$Read Joshua 24:14 to 24 (King James Version), where Joshua urges Israel to choose whom they will serve.

In English class, an argument is not a fight. It is a claim supported by reasons and evidence. The parts are the claim, which is the position; the reasons, which are why the position is right; the evidence, which is the facts, examples or testimony behind each reason; the counterclaim, which is what the other side says; and the rebuttal, which answers the counterclaim.

Joshua 24 is a model of argument. Joshua's claim in verse 15 is that Israel should choose to serve the LORD: as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD. His reasons include that God brought them out of Egypt, led them through the wilderness and gave them the land. Verses 19 and 20 raise a counterclaim, that the people cannot serve the LORD because He is holy and jealous. The people respond, and Joshua makes them witnesses against themselves, so the argument ends with a commitment.

Method: read for the position first, then ask why the speaker holds it, and what proof is given.

Your task: (1) Identify Joshua's claim. (2) List two reasons he gives. (3) Find one counterclaim and tell how it is answered.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.2.4$q$),(14,$q$Read Proverbs 6:6 to 11 (King James Version) and think about the claim it makes.

A strong claim is specific and debatable. "Sleep is good" is too weak. "Teenagers need at least eight hours of sleep to learn well" is a claim that can be argued. Good evidence is relevant, accurate and enough. Types of evidence include facts, statistics, examples, expert testimony and quotations. Always explain how the evidence supports the claim. That explanation is called reasoning, and it is what turns a list of facts into an argument.

Proverbs 6:6 to 11 argues that diligence leads to provision and laziness leads to want. The writer gives an example, the ant, which has no guide, overseer or ruler, yet prepares food in summer and gathers in harvest. Then comes a question, How long wilt thou sleep, O sluggard? The reasoning is that if a small creature plans ahead, a person should too, and the result of idleness is that poverty comes like a traveler.

Method for your own argument: write the claim, give two reasons, give evidence for each, and explain in a sentence how each piece of evidence proves the reason.

Your task: write a specific claim about homework, sleep or reading, and support it with two reasons. Give one piece of evidence for each reason.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.2.4, ELA.9.C.1.3$q$),(15,$q$Read Acts 26:1 to 29 (King James Version), Paul's defense before King Agrippa.

Rhetoric is the art of effective communication. Aristotle named three appeals a speaker can use. Ethos is the appeal to credibility and character. Pathos is the appeal to emotion. Logos is the appeal to logic and evidence. Strong speakers and writers use all three in balance. Too much emotion with no evidence feels like manipulation, and a list of facts with no credibility or feeling can fail to move anyone.

Paul begins with ethos. He says he is happy to speak before Agrippa because the king is expert in all customs and questions among the Jews, and he tells of his strict life as a Pharisee. He uses pathos when he describes how he once persecuted believers and then met Christ on the road to Damascus. He uses logos when he says that he is saying nothing but what Moses and the prophets said should come, and when he points out that these things were not done in a corner. Agrippa's response, Almost thou persuadest me to be a Christian, shows that the appeals had an effect.

Your task: find one example of ethos, one of pathos and one of logos in Paul's speech. For each, quote a few words and tell why it fits.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.3.4$q$),(16,$q$Read Proverbs 15 (King James Version).

Proverbs often use contrast. The wise and the foolish, the soft answer and the harsh word, the cheerful heart and the broken spirit stand side by side. This pattern, called antithesis, helps the reader see why one choice is better. Many proverbs make a compact claim and rely on shared experience as evidence. A claim in one verse is a short argument, and you can unpack it by asking what it says, why it might be true, and what kind of appeal it uses.

Take Proverbs 15:1, A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger. The claim is that gentle speech calms conflict and harsh speech inflames it. The evidence is common experience: most of us have seen an argument cool or flare depending on tone. The appeal is mostly logos, because it states a cause and effect pattern, with some pathos in the words wrath and anger.

Method: pick a verse, rewrite it as a plain claim, think of an example that proves it, and decide which appeal is strongest.

Your task: choose one verse from Proverbs 15, state its claim in your own words, give one example from life that supports it, and explain which appeal (ethos, pathos or logos) is strongest.

Florida standards: ELA.9.R.2.4, ELA.9.R.3.4$q$)) as v(p,b), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p;

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(v.b)) from (values (17,$q$Writing begins before the first sentence. Good writers plan. Choose a topic, know your audience and your purpose, gather ideas, and write a thesis statement.

A thesis states your main point in one or two sentences. In an expository essay it explains or analyzes a topic. In an argumentative essay it takes a position that someone could disagree with. A weak thesis announces a topic: "This essay is about reading." A strong thesis makes a point: "Daily reading builds the vocabulary, focus and imagination that students need to succeed." A strong thesis is specific, arguable and previews the reasons that follow.

After the thesis, make an outline. Under the thesis, list three main points, and under each point list evidence or examples. A simple outline keeps your essay organized and saves time while drafting. For instance: Thesis. Point one: reading builds vocabulary. Point two: reading builds focus. Point three: reading builds imagination. Each point gets one or two pieces of evidence.

Your task: choose one topic from this list: the value of daily reading, the importance of integrity, or whether students should do community service. Write a thesis and a three point outline with one piece of evidence under each point.

Florida standards: ELA.9.C.1.3, ELA.9.C.1.4$q$),(18,$q$A strong body paragraph does four jobs. It opens with a topic sentence that states the paragraph's main point. It gives evidence such as a fact, example or quotation. It adds reasoning that explains how the evidence proves the point. It closes with a sentence that wraps up the idea or leads to the next paragraph. Transitions such as first, for example, in addition, however and as a result connect ideas so the reader never gets lost.

Use the pattern claim, evidence, reasoning, sometimes called CER. Here is a sample. Claim: Daily reading builds vocabulary. Evidence: A student who reads twenty minutes a day meets thousands of new words in a year. Reasoning: Because meeting a word in context helps a reader learn its meaning, more reading means more words learned without extra memorizing. Closing: Vocabulary growth is the first benefit of a reading habit.

Common problems are paragraphs that list facts without explaining them, or that wander to a new topic. Check that every sentence belongs to your main point.

Your task: draft one body paragraph of six to eight sentences for your outline from the last lesson. Use the pattern claim, evidence, reasoning, and include at least one transition word.

Florida standards: ELA.9.C.1.3, ELA.9.C.1.4$q$),(19,$q$Revising means improving ideas, organization and style. It is different from editing, which fixes grammar and spelling. Revise first, then edit, because there is no point polishing a sentence you may delete.

Great writers seldom get it right the first time. They reread, ask for feedback from adults, peers or online editing tools, and improve their work for clarity and cohesiveness. Clarity means a reader can easily follow the meaning. Cohesiveness means the parts fit together and the ideas flow.

Use this revision checklist. Is my thesis clear? Does each paragraph support it? Is there enough evidence, and does my reasoning explain it? Are my transitions smooth? Is my voice consistent, neither too casual nor too stiff? Are there sentences that are vague, repeated or off topic? Try reading your paragraph aloud, since your ear will catch what your eyes miss. Try also cutting any sentence that does not help your point, and adding an example where a claim feels thin.

Your task: revise your paragraph from the last lesson using the checklist. Then write one sentence telling what you changed and why.

Florida standards: ELA.9.C.1.5$q$),(20,$q$Editing checks conventions: spelling, capitalization, punctuation, grammar and format. Publishing means preparing your final copy to share. Editing comes after revising, so ideas are settled before you polish the surface.

Use this editing checklist. Are all sentences complete, with a subject and a verb? Do subjects and verbs agree, as in "the students are" and "the student is"? Are the first word of each sentence and all proper nouns capitalized? Are commas used after introductory phrases, between items in a list and before conjunctions joining two independent clauses? Are apostrophes used correctly for possession and contractions? Are quotation marks placed correctly around quoted words? Is spelling correct, including words that sound alike such as their, there and they're? Is the tense consistent?

Read your paragraph backward, one sentence at a time, to catch errors, and use a dictionary or spell checker as a helper rather than the final judge. For publishing, add a title, check the format your assignment asks for, and read the final copy once more.

Your task: edit your revised paragraph using the checklist, and write the final copy with a title. List two errors you found and fixed.

Florida standards: ELA.9.C.1.5, ELA.9.C.3.1$q$),(21,$q$Context clues are hints in the surrounding words that help you figure out an unfamiliar word. You do not always need a dictionary. Learn five types. A definition clue states the meaning directly. A synonym clue uses a word with a similar meaning. An antonym clue uses an opposite, often with signals like but or unlike. An example clue gives examples that show the meaning. An inference clue asks you to put the whole sentence together and reason out the meaning.

Read Proverbs 1:1 to 7 (King James Version) and look at words like prudence, subtilty and discretion. Verse 4 says, To give subtilty to the simple, to the young man knowledge and discretion. The word subtilty here means shrewdness or good judgment, and the sentence helps because it is paired with knowledge and discretion, and it describes a gift to the simple, who need wisdom. Verse 7 gives an antonym clue: the fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge: but fools despise wisdom and instruction.

Method: read the whole sentence, find the unknown word, look for the clue type, make a guess, then check by putting your guess in place of the word to see if the sentence still makes sense.

Your task: choose three unfamiliar words from this unit's readings and use context clues to define them. Name the clue type for each.

Florida standards: ELA.9.V.1.3$q$),(22,$q$Words are built from parts. A root carries the core meaning. A prefix comes before the root and changes its meaning. A suffix comes after the root and often changes the part of speech. Knowing common parts lets you figure out thousands of words.

Useful parts: pre means before, re means again, un means not, bene means good, dict means speak, port means carry, tion means act of. Put them together. Predict means to say beforehand (pre plus dict). Benefit means a good result (bene plus fit). Transport means to carry across (trans plus port). Unhappy means not happy (un plus happy). Reaction means the act of acting again (re plus act plus ion).

Scripture words follow the same patterns. Benediction means a good saying, a blessing (bene plus dict plus ion). Revelation comes from a root meaning to uncover. Transgression means a stepping across a boundary. When you meet a long word, split it into parts, guess each part, and then guess the whole.

Your task: break down five words into prefix, root and suffix and give the meaning of each part and of the whole word. Choose at least one word from Scripture.

Florida standards: ELA.9.V.1.2$q$),(23,$q$Denotation is a word's dictionary meaning. Connotation is the feeling or association that comes with it. Words like slim, skinny and thin share a denotation, since all describe a lean body, but they carry different connotations. Slim feels positive, skinny can feel negative, and thin is neutral. Writers choose words with care because connotation shapes tone and persuades readers.

In Scripture and in speeches, word choice matters. Compare "house" and "home." Both name a place to live, but home carries warmth and belonging. Compare "stubborn" and "determined." One sounds like a fault and the other like a strength, though both describe a person who does not give up.

Academic vocabulary includes words used in school and professional writing: analyze, evidence, infer, evaluate, interpret, summarize, compare and contrast. These words appear in assignments, tests and essays, and knowing them makes tasks clearer. To analyze means to break something into parts to understand it. To infer means to reach a conclusion from clues. To evaluate means to judge quality or value.

Your task: write three pairs of words with similar meanings but different connotations, and use each in a sentence. Then use two academic vocabulary words correctly in sentences of your own.

Florida standards: ELA.9.V.1.1, ELA.9.V.1.3$q$),(24,$q$A phrase is a group of words without a subject and verb together, such as "in the morning" or "running down the road." A clause has a subject and a verb. An independent clause can stand alone as a sentence: The students read. A dependent clause cannot stand alone because it starts with a word like because, although, when or if: Because the students read.

A semicolon joins two closely related independent clauses. Example: The storm was fierce; the ship held fast. A colon introduces a list, a quotation or an explanation, but only after a complete sentence. Example: Bring three things: a pencil, a notebook and a Bible. Do not put a colon after a verb that needs its object, as in "I need: a pencil."

Correct a dependent clause by attaching it to an independent clause. Example: Because the students read every day, their vocabulary grew. When the dependent clause comes first, put a comma after it. Check your own sentences by asking, does this group of words have a subject and a verb, and does it make complete sense alone?

Your task: write two sentences using a semicolon, two using a colon, and one that uses a dependent clause correctly. Circle the clause or punctuation that each sentence is practicing.

Florida standards: ELA.9.C.3.1$q$)) as v(p,b), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p;

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (1,$q$Think it through: an expression is a sentence written in math. Read it piece by piece and say what each part does. In 25m + 60, the 25 grows with m, and the 60 never changes. Order of operations rules every evaluation: parentheses, exponents, multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction. A common mistake is to treat 3x² as (3x)². The exponent belongs only to x, so with x = 4 you square 4 first to get 16, then multiply by 3 to get 48. Writing each step on its own line keeps you from skipping one.$q$),(2,$q$Think it through: each law is a shortcut for writing out repeated multiplication. x² · x³ means (x · x)(x · x · x), which is five x factors, so x⁵. That is why you add exponents when you multiply and multiply exponents when you raise a power to a power. Compare x² · x³ = x⁵ with (x²)³ = x⁶. A common mistake is (2x³)² = 2x⁶. The 2 is also squared, so the answer is 4x⁶. A negative exponent does not make the number negative. It flips it: 2⁻³ = 1/8. Test any rule with small numbers: 2² · 2³ = 4 · 8 = 32, and 2⁵ = 32.$q$),(3,$q$Think it through: simplifying a radical means finding the largest perfect square hiding inside it. For √72, the largest perfect square factor is 36, so √72 = √36 · √2 = 6√2. You cannot add radicals with different insides, just as you cannot add 2 apples and 3 oranges into 5 apples. A common mistake is writing √2 + √3 = √5. Check with a calculator: 1.414 + 1.732 = 3.146, but √5 is about 2.236. Simplify every radical first, because sometimes unlike looking radicals turn out to be like radicals, as with √12 and √27.$q$),(4,$q$Think it through: add and subtract polynomials by combining like terms only. When you multiply, every term in the first polynomial must meet every term in the second. For (x + 4)(x − 2), the four products are x · x, x · (−2), 4 · x and 4 · (−2). The most common error is a sign mistake when subtracting. In (5x − 3) − (2x − 7), the minus sign changes both terms, so −(−7) becomes +7. You can always check an answer by choosing a value for x. Let x = 1: (1 + 4)(1 − 2) = −5, and x² + 2x − 8 = 1 + 2 − 8 = −5. They match.$q$),(5,$q$Think it through: an equation is a balanced scale. Whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other, and the goal is to get the variable alone. Work in this order: clear parentheses, combine like terms, gather the variable on one side, and then undo addition and subtraction before multiplication and division. Example: 2(x + 3) = 14. Divide both sides by 2 to get x + 3 = 7, so x = 4. Check: 2(4 + 3) = 14. The check takes ten seconds and catches most mistakes. For word problems, say what the variable stands for before you write the equation.$q$),(6,$q$Think it through: solving for a letter in a formula uses the same steps as solving any equation. The other letters simply stay in the answer as if they were numbers. Take A = ½bh and solve for h. Multiply both sides by 2 to get 2A = bh, then divide both sides by b to get h = 2A/b. A common mistake is dividing only part of one side. In P = 2l + 2w, after you subtract 2l you must divide the whole side by 2, so w = (P − 2l)/2. Formulas like these are tools. Builders, nurses and pilots rearrange them every day.$q$),(7,$q$Think it through: an inequality has many solutions, so the answer is a range of numbers rather than one value. The sign flips only when you multiply or divide by a negative number. Test your answer with real numbers. For −2x ≤ 8 we found x ≥ −4. Try x = 0: −2(0) = 0, and 0 ≤ 8 is true, and 0 is in the answer. Try x = −5: −2(−5) = 10, and 10 ≤ 8 is false, and −5 is not in the answer. Both tests agree. In a word problem, read the answer in context. Seven and a half books makes no sense, so the answer is 7 books.$q$),(8,$q$Think it through: absolute value measures distance from zero, and distance is never negative. So |x − 3| = 5 asks which numbers are 5 units away from 3. Count 5 to the right to get 8 and 5 to the left to get −2. That is why there are two answers. If the problem says |x| = −4, there is no solution, because distance cannot be negative. Always isolate the absolute value expression first, then split into two equations. Check both answers in the original equation. On a graph, the vertex of y = |x − h| + k is the point where the V turns, at (h, k).$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='algebra-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Think it through:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (9,$q$Think it through: picture a vending machine. Press one button and you get exactly one item. That is a function. If one button could give two different items, you could not predict the result, and that is not a function. Domain is what you may put in, and range is what comes out. A common mistake is reading f(x) as f times x. It means the output when the input is x. Another is squaring a negative incorrectly. For g(x) = x² + 1, g(−3) = (−3)² + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10. Use parentheses around negative inputs every time.$q$),(10,$q$Think it through: slope answers the question, how much does y change when x increases by one? Always subtract in the same order on top and bottom. For (1, 5) and (4, −1), m = (−1 − 5)/(4 − 1) = −6/3 = −2. Mixing the order flips the sign and gives the wrong answer. A rate of change always has units. A car going 90 miles in 1.5 hours has a slope of 60 miles per hour. A horizontal line has slope 0, because nothing changes. A vertical line has undefined slope, because the run is 0 and you cannot divide by 0.$q$),(11,$q$Think it through: choose the form that fits what you are given. If you know the slope and the y intercept, use y = mx + b. If you know a point and the slope, use point slope form. If you know two points, find the slope first, then use either point. Always check your line by plugging both points into the final equation. For the line through (0, 1) and (2, 5), we found y = 2x + 1. Check: 2(2) + 1 = 5, which matches the second point. Remember that perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals, so 2 and −1/2 go together.$q$),(12,$q$Think it through: two clues in a word problem point to the slope and the intercept. The word per, as in per week or per gigabyte, signals the slope. A starting amount or a flat fee signals the y intercept. In y = 15x + 120, the 15 means 15 dollars saved each week, and the 120 means 120 dollars at the start. Always explain your answer with units and context. Also ask what values make sense. You cannot sell negative tickets, so the domain starts at 0 and counts whole numbers. When two plans are compared, set them equal to find the point where their costs match.$q$),(13,$q$Think it through: the boundary line splits the plane into two sides, and the test point tells you which side to shade. Use (0, 0) when the line does not pass through it, because the arithmetic is easy. If the line does pass through the origin, test another point such as (1, 0). A solid line means points on the line count, so it is used with ≤ and ≥. A dashed line means they do not count, so it is used with < and >. For y ≥ 3x + 2, draw a solid line through (0, 2) with slope 3, and test (0, 0): 0 ≥ 2 is false, so shade the side away from the origin.$q$),(14,$q$Think it through: graphing gives a picture of the solution, but pictures can be a little off. Always confirm the intersection by substituting into both equations. For (4, 3) in x + y = 7 and x − y = 1, we get 4 + 3 = 7 and 4 − 3 = 1, so both are true. Lines with the same slope and different intercepts never meet, so the system has no solution. Two equations that describe the same line, like y = 3x + 1 and 2y = 6x + 2, share every point, so there are infinitely many solutions. Write each equation in slope intercept form to compare them quickly.$q$),(15,$q$Think it through: choose the method that fits. When one equation already has a variable alone, as in y = 2x − 1, use substitution. When the equations line up with matching or opposite coefficients, use elimination. Put the whole expression in parentheses when you substitute, so signs do not get lost. Example of elimination: x + y = 9 and x − y = 1. Add the equations to get 2x = 10, so x = 5, and then y = 4. Always check the pair in both original equations. In a word problem, name your two unknowns first, for example a for adults and c for children.$q$),(16,$q$Think it through: a system of inequalities describes a set of rules that must all be true at once. The solution is the overlap of the shaded regions. To test a point, check it in every inequality. Real situations add hidden rules. You cannot buy a negative number of notebooks, so n ≥ 0 and p ≥ 0. And you usually buy whole items, so only whole number points count. In the notebook problem, n = 6 and p = 3 gives 3(6) + 2(3) = 24, which exactly uses the budget. A viable option must satisfy all the inequalities and make sense in the story.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='algebra-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Think it through:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (17,$q$Think it through: factoring is multiplication run backward, so you can always check by multiplying. Follow this order. First pull out any greatest common factor. Then look at the number of terms. A binomial that is a difference of squares factors as (A − B)(A + B). A trinomial needs two numbers that multiply to c and add to b. A sign hint: if c is positive, both numbers share the sign of b. If c is negative, the numbers have opposite signs. Check: (x + 3)(x + 4) = x² + 4x + 3x + 12 = x² + 7x + 12, which matches.$q$),(18,$q$Think it through: choose the method that fits the equation. If it factors easily, factor. If it looks like (x − h)² = k, take square roots and remember the plus or minus. Otherwise use the quadratic formula, which always works. Before you factor, set the equation equal to zero. The zero product property says that if a product equals zero, one factor must be zero, and it works only when the product is zero. So x² + x = 6 cannot be solved by writing x(x + 1) = 6 and setting x = 6. Move the 6 first: x² + x − 6 = 0. Check each solution in the original equation.$q$),(19,$q$Think it through: to graph a parabola, follow four steps. First decide whether it opens up or down from the sign of a. Second find the axis x = −b/(2a). Third find the vertex by substituting that x value. Fourth find the intercepts. Be careful with signs: for y = x² − 4x + 3, b is −4, so the axis is x = 4/2 = 2. A parabola is symmetric, so every point has a mirror image across the axis. The y intercept (0, 3) has a partner at (4, 3), two units on each side of x = 2. Use these mirror points to sketch a smooth curve.$q$),(20,$q$Think it through: quadratic models answer questions about the highest point, the lowest point, and when something hits zero. The vertex gives the maximum when the parabola opens down. In h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 5, the number 16 comes from gravity measured in feet and seconds. Read your answers with units: 2 seconds and 69 feet. When you solve for the landing time, you may get a negative value as well as a positive one. Time cannot be negative here, so reject it and keep the positive answer. Always ask whether an answer makes sense in the story.$q$),(21,$q$Think it through: exponential change multiplies, and that is why it grows so fast. In 1000(1.05)², you multiply by 1.05 twice, which is not the same as 1000 · 1.05 · 2. To build a growth factor from a rate, add the rate to 1, so 7 percent growth uses 1.07. For decay, subtract it from 1, so 7 percent decay uses 0.93. The parable of the talents in Matthew 25, verses 14 to 30, teaches faithful use of what has been entrusted to us. Understanding how money grows over time is part of wise stewardship, and compound interest is a tool that rewards patience and discipline.$q$),(22,$q$Think it through: transformations move a graph without changing its basic shape. The rule that surprises most students is the horizontal shift. In (x + 4)², the plus sign moves the graph left, not right, because the vertex occurs where x + 4 = 0, which is x = −4. Vertical changes behave as you expect: adding 3 lifts the graph 3 units. Comparing models, a line may start far ahead, but an exponential with a base greater than 1 will eventually pass it. When you compare 100x and 2ˣ, make a table of values for x = 1 through 12 and look for where the exponential first becomes larger.$q$),(23,$q$Think it through: no single number describes a data set perfectly, so choose the one that fits. The mean uses every value, so a very large or very small value pulls it. The median is the middle value, so it stays steady. News reports on household income usually use the median, because a few extremely high incomes would pull the mean upward and give a false picture of a typical family. Use the IQR to describe spread when you use the median. A box plot shows the five number summary at a glance: the box holds the middle half of the data, and a long whisker shows a stretched out tail.$q$),(24,$q$Think it through: a strong correlation means two quantities move together. It does not mean one causes the other. Ice cream sales and sunburns both rise in summer, but ice cream does not cause sunburn. The warm weather is the hidden cause. A line of best fit lets you make predictions inside your data, called interpolation, with reasonable confidence. Predictions far outside the data, called extrapolation, can fail badly, as the 184 point test score showed. A residual measures how far a real point sits from the line, so small residuals mean the line fits well. Always explain slope and intercept in the words of the story.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='algebra-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Think it through:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (1,$q$Go deeper: notice how Genesis 1 and 2 answer different questions. Chapter 1 shows God's power and order, and chapter 2 shows His personal care for the man and the woman He made. The fall in chapter 3 is a choice to doubt what God said, and every sin since has begun in the same way. Yet God does not abandon Adam and Eve. He clothes them and gives a promise. As you read, ask what the story teaches about who God is, who we are, and why the world needs a Savior.$q$),(2,$q$Go deeper: Abraham is not remembered for being perfect. He is remembered for believing God. Genesis 15:6 says he believed in the LORD, and it was counted to him for righteousness, and the New Testament returns to that verse to teach that faith has always been the way to please God. The story of Joseph shows the same trust over a long road: betrayal, prison and promotion did not defeat God's plan. As you study, trace how one promise is handed down through one family, because that thread leads to Christ.$q$),(3,$q$Go deeper: the Passover and the Exodus are the Old Testament's great picture of salvation. The blood on the doorposts, the crossing of the sea and the giving of the law show a God who rescues first and then instructs. Israel was brought out of Egypt before the commandments were given at Sinai, so obedience is the response of a redeemed people, not the price of rescue. Ask yourself what the Passover lamb teaches, and keep the question in mind until you reach John 1.$q$),(4,$q$Go deeper: Leviticus can feel distant, but it teaches that a holy God can dwell among sinful people only through atonement. The tabernacle, the priests and the sacrifices all say that sin is serious and that God provides a way to be near Him. Numbers 6 gives the priestly blessing that Israel heard for generations. Deuteronomy 6 gives the heart of the law, to love the LORD with all the heart, soul and might, and to teach it to the children. Read each book as God showing His people how to live with Him.$q$),(5,$q$Go deeper: Joshua opens with a command repeated more than once, Be strong and of a good courage, and the courage rests on God's presence and His word, not on Joshua's ability. The fall of Jericho shows that victory came by obedience to God's instructions. Joshua 24 ends with a choice that every generation must make. Notice that Joshua leads by example before he asks the people to follow, saying that he and his house will serve the LORD.$q$),(6,$q$Go deeper: the Book of Judges repeats a sad cycle: the people sin, they are oppressed, they cry out, and God raises up a deliverer. The last verse says that every man did that which was right in his own eyes, which explains why the nation was in trouble. Ruth shines against that dark background. A foreign widow shows loyalty and faith, and Boaz acts as a kinsman redeemer. Ruth becomes part of the line of David, and so of the line of Christ.$q$),(7,$q$Go deeper: Israel asked for a king, and Samuel, Saul, David and Solomon show both the promise and the problem of earthly kingship. Saul's pride led to his rejection. David was a man after God's own heart, though he was not without sin, and God made him a covenant promise in 2 Samuel 7 that his throne would be established forever. Solomon asked for wisdom and built the temple. Watch for how the covenant with David points beyond David to a greater king.$q$),(8,$q$Go deeper: after Solomon the kingdom divided, and both the north and the south drifted into idolatry despite many prophets who warned them. The exile was judgment, but it was never the end of God's purpose. A remnant returned. Ezra taught the law, Nehemiah rebuilt the wall, and in Esther God's hand is seen working through events without His name being spoken. Ask what these books teach about God's faithfulness when His people are faithless.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='bib-101' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Go deeper:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (9,$q$Go deeper: Job is a book about suffering and trust. Job loses nearly everything, and his friends insist that his pain must be punishment for sin. Job does not understand, but he does not curse God, and he cries out for a redeemer in chapter 19. In the end God speaks, and Job learns that God's wisdom is greater than his questions. The book does not explain every reason for pain. It teaches us to keep trusting a God who is good and who is in control.$q$),(10,$q$Go deeper: the Psalms teach us how to talk to God. Some are songs of praise, some are laments full of honest pain, and some are prayers of trust. Hebrew poetry uses parallelism, where a second line repeats, contrasts or deepens the first. Psalm 1 contrasts two ways of living, Psalm 19 says that creation and the word of God both speak, Psalm 23 pictures God as shepherd, and Psalm 100 invites everyone to thank Him. Read them aloud and make them part of your own prayers.$q$),(11,$q$Go deeper: Proverbs teaches wisdom for everyday life, and its starting point is stated in chapter 1: the fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge. Wisdom shows up in the way we speak, work, handle money and choose friends. Proverbs speaks in general principles, not in unbreakable promises, so it must be read with the whole of Scripture. Pick one proverb from each chapter you read and ask how it should change what you do this week.$q$),(12,$q$Go deeper: Ecclesiastes asks what life means when everything is seen only under the sun. The Preacher finds that work, pleasure and wealth cannot satisfy by themselves, and he calls it vanity. His conclusion in chapter 12 is clear: fear God and keep His commandments. Song of Solomon celebrates faithful married love as God's gift, and many readers also see in it a picture of the love between God and His people. Together these books teach us to receive life as a gift from God.$q$),(13,$q$Go deeper: Isaiah's call begins with a vision of the holiness of God, and only then does he hear the question, Whom shall I send? Seeing God's holiness leads to confession, cleansing and service. The book moves from judgment to comfort, and chapters 7, 9, 53 and 61 look ahead to the Messiah. The New Testament quotes Isaiah often. As you read, list every promise that Christians believe was fulfilled in Jesus.$q$),(14,$q$Go deeper: Jeremiah was called while young and served for decades with little visible success. He preached repentance, warned of Babylon, and wept over the people's sin. Yet his book carries hope: in chapter 29 God tells the exiles to settle, to seek the peace of the city, and to trust that He has plans for them. In chapter 31 He promises a new covenant written on the heart. Lamentations teaches that you can grieve honestly and still say that God's mercies are new every morning.$q$),(15,$q$Go deeper: Ezekiel served among the exiles in Babylon, and he saw the glory of God there, which taught the people that God was not confined to the temple in Jerusalem. He was a watchman who was responsible to warn. His book also carries promises: God Himself will shepherd His flock, give His people a new heart and a new spirit, and breathe life into the dry bones. Ask what each of these promises shows about God's power to restore what looks dead.$q$),(16,$q$Go deeper: Daniel and his friends lived faithfully in a foreign empire. They refused to defile themselves, they refused to bow to the image, and Daniel kept praying even when it was forbidden. Each time God was with them in the trial. The visions in the second half of the book show that God rules over the kingdoms of the world and that His kingdom will last forever. Ask what it costs to live with integrity where the culture does not share your faith.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='bib-101' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Go deeper:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (17,$q$Go deeper: Hosea shows God's faithful love through a prophet who is told to love an unfaithful wife, a picture of how God loves a straying people. Joel calls for a return to God with the whole heart and looks ahead to the pouring out of the Spirit, which Peter later explains in Acts 2. Amos cries out for justice and righteousness and warns that worship without a just life is empty. Each prophet calls the people back to the covenant.$q$),(18,$q$Go deeper: Obadiah warns that pride brings a fall. Jonah is a story about the mercy of God. The prophet runs away, God sends a storm and a great fish, and in the end Nineveh repents. The book ends with a question about God's compassion, and it asks us to look at our own hearts. Micah 6:8 sums up what the LORD requires: to do justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with God. Micah 5:2 names Bethlehem as the birthplace of the ruler to come.$q$),(19,$q$Go deeper: these three prophets teach us how to trust God when evil seems to win. Nahum shows that God is good and that He will judge Nineveh. Habakkuk asks honest questions, how long and why, and God answers that the just shall live by his faith. The book ends with a song of joy that does not depend on circumstances. Zephaniah promises that the LORD in the midst of His people will rejoice over them with singing. Ask how you can bring hard questions to God and still worship.$q$),(20,$q$Go deeper: after returning from exile, the people left the temple unfinished while they built their own houses. Haggai challenged their priorities, and Zechariah reminded them that the work would be done not by might nor by power, but by the Spirit of the LORD. Zechariah 9:9 pictures a king coming humble and riding on a donkey. Malachi closes the Old Testament by promising a messenger who will prepare the way. The prophets leave us waiting, which prepares us for the New Testament.$q$),(21,$q$Go deeper: the Bible is one story, and the covenants are its thread. God promised Abraham a blessing for all nations, promised David an everlasting throne, and promised through Jeremiah a new covenant written on the heart. In Luke 24 the risen Jesus shows His disciples that all the Scriptures speak of Him. When you read the Old Testament, look for how each covenant promise comes closer to its fulfillment, and then see how the New Testament names that fulfillment.$q$),(22,$q$Go deeper: the prophecies give details written long before the events. Isaiah 7 speaks of a virgin and a son named Immanuel. Micah 5 names Bethlehem. Isaiah 53 describes a servant who bears our griefs and is wounded for our transgressions. Psalm 22 describes a death by suffering, and Psalm 110 speaks of one who is both king and priest. Read them slowly and record which details the Gospels report. Then notice that the fulfillment was not forced, since many of these writings were separated from Christ by centuries.$q$),(23,$q$Go deeper: sacrifice, priesthood and the coming prophet all point to Christ. Isaac on Mount Moriah is spared when God provides a ram, which teaches that God provides the offering. Leviticus 17:11 says the life of the flesh is in the blood and that blood makes atonement. The high priest entered on the Day of Atonement once a year. John 1:29 declares Jesus the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world. Ask how the Old Testament system prepared people to understand what Jesus did.$q$),(24,$q$Go deeper: the four hundred years between Malachi and Matthew are sometimes called the silent years, but God was preparing the world. Many people spoke one common language, Roman roads made travel easier, and many hearts were waiting. Galatians 4:4 says that when the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth His Son. Isaiah 40 and Malachi 3 promise a messenger who prepares the way, and the New Testament says John the Baptist was that messenger.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='bib-101' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Go deeper:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (1,$q$Go deeper: each Gospel presents Jesus to a different audience. Matthew writes especially to show how Jesus fulfills the Old Testament, Mark presents Him as the active servant, Luke shows His compassion for all people, and John declares that the Word was God and was made flesh. The birth narratives show that God kept His promise in a humble way, with a manger and shepherds. Ask why God chose to come as a child, and what that tells you about His love.$q$),(2,$q$Go deeper: Jesus was baptized, and then He was tempted. Each time the devil tempted Him, Jesus answered with the written word, saying, It is written. That is a model for us. The Sermon on the Mount describes the character of the kingdom, from the Beatitudes to prayer, giving, forgiving and trusting the Father. Jesus calls disciples to a righteousness that begins in the heart. Choose one teaching from chapters 5 and 6 and plan how to practice it this week.$q$),(3,$q$Go deeper: the miracles of Jesus show His authority over storms, demons, sickness and even death. They are signs that point to who He is, and each one is also an act of compassion. His parables use ordinary things, like seeds, sheep and sons, to teach about the kingdom. In Luke 15 the lost sheep, coin and son show a God who searches and rejoices. The raising of Lazarus in John 11 points ahead to Jesus's own resurrection, as He says, I am the resurrection, and the life.$q$),(4,$q$Go deeper: the Gospels give much space to the last week of Jesus's life because the cross and the resurrection are the center of the gospel. He was crucified in the place of sinners, and He cried out that it was finished. John 3:16 gives the reason, that God so loved the world. On the third day the tomb was empty and Jesus appeared to His disciples. In Matthew 28 He commissions them to go and teach all nations. The resurrection proves that He is who He said He was.$q$),(5,$q$Go deeper: Acts begins where Luke ends. Jesus promised that His disciples would receive power after the Holy Ghost came upon them, and that they would be witnesses in Jerusalem, Judea, Samaria and to the uttermost part of the earth. On the day of Pentecost the Spirit was poured out, and Peter preached that the people should repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and receive the gift of the Holy Ghost. About three thousand were added that day.$q$),(6,$q$Go deeper: the early church grew through bold witness. In Acts 3 a lame man is healed in the name of Jesus, and Peter explains that the power came from Him. When the leaders threatened them, the apostles answered that there is no other name under heaven given among men whereby we must be saved. Stephen became the first martyr, and Saul stood by approving. Then Saul met Jesus on the road to Damascus and was changed. The persecutor became a preacher, which shows what God's grace can do.$q$),(7,$q$Go deeper: Acts 10 is a turning point. God shows Peter in a vision that he should not call any person common or unclean, and Cornelius, a Gentile, receives the Holy Ghost as the Jews had. The church at Antioch sends out Barnabas and Saul on the first missionary journey. In Acts 15 the council in Jerusalem agrees that Gentiles are saved by grace, not by keeping the law of Moses. Ask what this teaches about the church welcoming all kinds of people.$q$),(8,$q$Go deeper: Paul's journeys carried the gospel across the Roman world. In Philippi a jailer and his household believe after an earthquake. In Berea the people searched the Scriptures daily to see whether Paul's message was true, which is a good example for every student. At Athens Paul preached to philosophers. In Acts 20 he says farewell to the Ephesian elders, and Acts ends with Paul preaching in Rome, unhindered. Notice that he kept preaching through prison and hardship.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='bib-102' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Go deeper:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (9,$q$Go deeper: Romans lays out the gospel in order. All have sinned and come short of the glory of God. We are justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus. Chapter 5 says that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us. Chapter 6 asks whether grace gives us license to sin, and answers no, because we have been raised to walk in newness of life. Read slowly and write down each step of Paul's argument.$q$),(10,$q$Go deeper: Romans 8 begins with no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus and ends with the assurance that nothing can separate us from the love of God. The Spirit leads, helps us pray and testifies that we are children of God. Romans 10 says that whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved, and asks how they will hear without a preacher. Romans 12 calls us to present our bodies as a living sacrifice and to be transformed by the renewing of our minds.$q$),(11,$q$Go deeper: the Corinthian church had gifts, but it also had divisions. In 1 Corinthians 12 Paul teaches that the church is one body with many members, and each one is needed. Chapter 13 shows that without love, even great gifts count for nothing. Chapter 15 defends the resurrection as the foundation of the faith. In 2 Corinthians 5 he says that if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature, and in chapter 12 he learns that God's grace is sufficient in weakness.$q$),(12,$q$Go deeper: Galatians was written to churches that were being told they must add the works of the law to faith in Christ. Paul answers that a man is justified by the faith of Jesus Christ, not by the works of the law, and that Christ has made us free. He warns against using that freedom as an occasion for the flesh. Instead we are to serve one another in love and walk in the Spirit. The fruit of the Spirit in chapter 5 describes what a Spirit led life looks like.$q$),(13,$q$Go deeper: Ephesians moves from what God has done to how we should live. We are saved by grace through faith, it is the gift of God, and we are His workmanship created unto good works. Chapter 4 calls the church to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace. Chapter 6 describes the armor of God, from the belt of truth to the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God. Choose one piece of armor and describe how you will use it this week.$q$),(14,$q$Go deeper: Paul wrote Philippians from prison, yet the letter is full of joy. In chapter 2 he points to the humility of Christ, who took the form of a servant, and in chapter 4 he says that he has learned to be content in every state and that he can do all things through Christ who strengthens him. Colossians declares that Christ has the preeminence in all things. Chapter 3 teaches us to set our affections on things above and to put on love.$q$),(15,$q$Go deeper: Paul wrote to the Thessalonians to encourage a young church that faced pressure. He comforts them about those who have died in Christ and teaches that the Lord Himself shall descend from heaven, and so shall we ever be with the Lord. He also tells them to comfort one another with these words. Chapter 5 urges them to be watchful and to rejoice evermore and pray without ceasing. In 2 Thessalonians 3 he reminds them to keep working faithfully while they wait.$q$),(16,$q$Go deeper: Paul's letters to Timothy and Titus are written to young pastors. He urges Timothy to be an example of the believers in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith and in purity. In 2 Timothy 3:16 he teaches that all scripture is given by inspiration of God and is profitable for doctrine, reproof, correction and instruction in righteousness. Titus 2 teaches sound doctrine and good works. Philemon is a short letter that shows how the gospel changes a relationship, as Paul asks Philemon to receive his runaway servant Onesimus as a brother.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='bib-102' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Go deeper:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', E'\n\nYour task:', E'\n\n'||v.a||E'\n\nYour task:'))) from (values (17,$q$Go deeper: Hebrews shows that Jesus is better than the prophets, the angels, Moses and the Levitical priests. He is our great high priest who can be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, so we may come boldly to the throne of grace. Chapter 11 recounts the heroes of faith, who obtained a good report through faith. Chapter 12 tells us to lay aside every weight and the sin that so easily besets us, and to run with patience the race that is set before us, looking unto Jesus.$q$),(18,$q$Go deeper: James is practical. He says to count it all joy when you fall into diverse temptations, because the trying of your faith worketh patience. He promises that anyone who lacks wisdom may ask of God. He warns that faith without works is dead, which means that real faith shows itself in action. He also warns about the power of the tongue and urges humility. Choose one command from James and write how you will obey it this week.$q$),(19,$q$Go deeper: Peter writes to believers who suffer. He opens with praise that God has begotten us again unto a lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ. He calls the church a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation. He tells elders to feed the flock and tells all to cast their care upon God, because He cares for us. In 2 Peter 3 he reminds us that the Lord is not slack concerning His promise, and he urges us to grow in grace and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ.$q$),(20,$q$Go deeper: John writes about light and love. If we walk in the light, as He is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ cleanseth us from all sin. If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us. Chapter 4 says that God is love, and that we love because He first loved us. Chapter 5 says that whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world. Jude urges believers to earnestly contend for the faith which was once delivered unto the saints.$q$),(21,$q$Go deeper: Revelation begins with John on the isle of Patmos, in exile, seeing the risen Christ among the lampstands. The letters to the seven churches in chapters 2 and 3 show how Jesus knows His church. He commends faithfulness, corrects error, and calls each church to repent or hold fast. Each letter ends with a promise to him that overcometh. As you read, ask what Christ praises, what He corrects, and which letter speaks most directly to churches today.$q$),(22,$q$Go deeper: Revelation 4 and 5 give a view of heaven. The throne is at the center, and all heaven worships the One who sits on it. A scroll that no one else can open is opened by the Lamb that was slain, and the worship that follows declares that He is worthy. Chapter 7 shows a great multitude from every nation, kindred, people and tongue standing before the throne. Even in a book about judgment, worship is at the center.$q$),(23,$q$Go deeper: Revelation 12 shows that believers overcame the accuser by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony. In chapter 19 Christ comes as Faithful and True, with a name written, King of kings and Lord of lords. Chapter 20 describes the final judgment, when the dead are judged and evil is put away. The message to believers is not fear but confidence, because the Lord has won the victory. Faithfulness now is shaped by the certainty of that end.$q$),(24,$q$Go deeper: the Bible ends with a promise. Revelation 21 describes a new heaven and a new earth, where God dwells with His people and wipes away all tears from their eyes. There is no more death, sorrow or crying. The One on the throne says, Behold, I make all things new. Revelation 22 offers the water of life freely and ends with an invitation: whosoever will, let him take the water of life freely. Close the course by asking how this hope shapes the way you live today.$q$)) as v(p,a), courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='bib-102' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=v.p and i.content->>'body' not like '%Go deeper:%';

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}',to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body','Find the first whole number x where 2ˣ is larger than 100x.','Find the first whole number x greater than 0 where 2ˣ is larger than 100x.'))) from courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='algebra-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=22;

update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(jsonb_set(i.content,'{readings}','[{"title":"Genesis 41 (King James Version)","url":"/portal/bible/Genesis/41"}]'::jsonb),'{body}',to_jsonb('Practice reading: Genesis 41 (King James Version) is a clear example of problem and solution. As you read, name the problem, the solution Joseph proposes, and the result.'||E'\n\n'||(i.content->>'body'))) from courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-2' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=9 and jsonb_array_length(i.content->'readings')=0;
